documenting the transition isn't easy  but there's mounting evidence already  suggesting the new taliban is very much  like the old  human rights group amnesty international  has documented through photographic and  eyewitness testimony a massacre of nine  hazara men six were shot and three  others were tortured to death  they were killed between the 4th and 6th  of july in the eastern province of  ghazni the hazara have long faced  persecution in afghanistan and amnesty  says the killings  are a horrific indicator of what taliban  rule may bring particularly for  minorities  there's also evidence the taliban's  rounding up afghans who worked with the  fallen governments or u.s and british  forces one example mentioned in a u.n  intelligence report happened on tuesday  this week  men believed to be the taliban went to  the home of a former counter-terrorism  officer in kabul  and they gave him this letter  it said you're asked to present yourself  at the military and intelligence  commission and provide information in  relation to your cooperation with the  americans and the british should you  fail to attend at the above mentioned  address  members of your family will be arrested  and you'll have no right to complain and  you would carry the responsibility  now the report author says it's an  extremely worrying development  they directly went to the apartments of  these individuals  if they didn't arrest them they  they would go for their families instead  and as i've come out in the media also  uh in the last 24 hours uh incidences of  of of uh  killings of family members uh to other  journalists to interpreters and and  others so  we can expect these things to uh to be  happening uh most likely at a more  systematic scale ahead  what is different about the new taliban  is an awareness of their image abroad  something we've not seen before there is  no huge skepticism about their carefully  chosen words and their new presentation  governments around the world are  struggling to understand what exactly it  all means and form coherent foreign  policies  but the targeted killings and the  treatment of women in some areas  suggests the projection of a reformed  taliban  may just be for the cameras
 

